All relevant merge-key fixtures (valid merges, duplicate-merge-key override semantics, and invalid-merge error cases) behave correctly with the fix, and mocha/eslint aren't installed in this environment (no `node_modules`) so the full suite can't be run, but manual verification against the exact test fixtures covers the affected code path.

**Root cause:** `lib/loader.js:300` (`mergeMappings`) had its condition inverted — it only copied a key from the merge source into the destination when the destination *already had* that key, i.e. it never actually merged anything into an empty/new mapping.

**Fix:** negated the condition so a key is copied when the destination does *not* already have it:
```js
if (!_hasOwnProperty.call(destination, key)) {
  destination[key] = source[key];
  overridableKeys[key] = true;
}
```

Verified `yaml.load('base: &b {a: 1, b: 2}\nx:\n  <<: *b\n  c: 3\n').x` now returns `{ a: 1, b: 2, c: 3 }`, and the `construct-merge`, `duplicate-merge-key`, and `invalid-merge-1/2` fixtures all pass/throw as their test files expect.
